1. Scheduling & Placement:
    - These three concepts—Node Affinity, Pod Affinity, and Taints & Tolerations—are all about controlling where Kubernetes puts your Pods (i.e., scheduling decisions).
    - Think of Kubernetes as a “smart delivery system” placing containers (Pods) onto machines (Nodes). These rules help you influence that placement.

2. Node Affinity (choose a type of machine):
    - Node Affinity is like saying: “I want my app to run only on certain types of machines.”
    - For example:
        “Run this Pod only on high-memory nodes”
        “Run this only on Linux nodes in a specific zone”
    - Simple analogy
    Like ordering food and saying:
        “Deliver only if the rider has a bike, not a cycle.”
    - How it works
    Nodes have labels like:
        disktype=ssd
        zone=ap-south-1
        gpu=true
    You tell Kubernetes:
        “Place my Pod only on nodes with these labels.”
    - Types
        Required (hard rule) → Pod MUST go there or not run
        Preferred (soft rule) → Try to go there, but not mandatory

3. Pod Affinity (place Pods near each other):
    - Pod Affinity is about Pods, not machines.
    - It means: “Place this Pod near another Pod.”
    - Example use case
        You have:
            Backend API Pod
            Database Pod
        You want: “Keep backend close to database for faster communication.”
    - Simple analogy
        Like saying: “Seat my friends near each other in a movie theatre.”
    - Why it’s used
        Low latency communication
        Better performance
        Same zone / same node grouping
    - There is also: Pod Anti-Affinity → “Don’t place these Pods together” (for high availability)
    - Example: Don’t put two replicas of a database on the same node.

4. Taints & Tolerations (keep unwanted Pods out)
    - This is the most “security gate” style concept.
    - Taint (on Node)
        A Node says: “I will NOT accept Pods unless they explicitly say they are allowed.”
    - Example:
        Node = GPU machine
        Taint = “Only GPU workloads allowed”
    - Toleration (on Pod)
    Pod says: “I am allowed to be here despite the restriction.”
    - Simple analogy
        Imagine a VIP club:
            Taint = Bouncer at the door (“No entry unless VIP”)
            Toleration = VIP pass
    - Why it's useful
        Keep special workloads on special nodes (GPU, memory-heavy)
        Prevent normal apps from landing on critical nodes
        Isolate system workloads

5. How they differ (very important):
    Concept	Focus	        Controls
    Node Affinity	        Node selection	“Where should my Pod go?”
    Pod Affinity	        Pod placement near other Pods	“Which Pods should be together?”
    Taints & Tolerations	Node exclusion	“Who is NOT allowed here unless permitted?”